    "title": "<p>ROM and diagnostic management options for FNAB of lymph nodes</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Dr Sharron Liang, St Vincent's Hospital, Sydney, Australia",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 708.7pt; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"945\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 3.5pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 83.4pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; mso-background-themecolor: text2; mso-background-themetint: 102; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"111\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Diagnostic category</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 105.15pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; mso-background-themecolor: text2; mso-background-themetint: 102; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"140\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center; page-break-after: avoid;\" align=\"center\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Estimated <span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">ROM<sup>a</sup></span></span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 520.15pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; mso-background-themecolor: text2; mso-background-themetint: 102; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"694\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Diagnostic management <span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">options<sup>b,c</sup></span></span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 83.4pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"111\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\">Inadequate/​Insufficient</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 105.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"140\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">17&ndash;67% (34%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 520.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"694\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">CORRELATE<sup>d</sup>.</span> Repeat FNAB, ideally with ROSE and ancillary tests, and consider performing CNB.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 83.4pt; background: #DAEEF3; mso-background-themecolor: accent5; mso-background-themetint: 51; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"111\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Benign</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 105.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; mso-background-themecolor: accent5; mso-background-themetint: 51; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"140\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">2&ndash;16% (5%)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 520.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; mso-background-themecolor: accent5; mso-background-themetint: 51; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"694\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext; mso-no-proof: yes;\">CORRELATE<sup>d</sup>.</span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\"> If the findings correlate with the FNAB, review clinically as required. If the findings are discrepant with the FNAB, consider repeating the FNAB, ideally with ROSE and ancillary testing, and/or perform CNB.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 83.4pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"111\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\">Atypical</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 105.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"140\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">29&ndash;77% (65%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 520.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"694\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">CORRELATE<sup>d</sup>.</span> Consider repeating the FNAB, ideally with ROSE and ancillary testing, and/or perform CNB or excision biopsy.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 4; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 83.4pt; background: #DAEEF3; mso-background-themecolor: accent5; mso-background-themetint: 51; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"111\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Suspicious for malignancy</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 105.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; mso-background-themecolor: accent5; mso-background-themetint: 51; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"140\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">88&ndash;100% (93%)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 520.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; mso-background-themecolor: accent5; mso-background-themetint: 51; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"694\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext; mso-no-proof: yes;\">CORRELATE<sup>d</sup>.</span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\"> Repeat the FNAB, ideally with ROSE and ancillary testing, and/or perform CNB or excision <span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">biopsy<sup>e,f</sup></span>.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 5; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 83.4pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"111\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\">Malignant</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 105.15pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"140\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"text-align: center;\" align=\"center\">98&ndash;100% (100%)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 520.15pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"694\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">CORRELATE<sup>d</sup>.</span> If the findings are discrepant with the FNAB or more material is required for ancillary testing, repeat the FNAB, ideally with ROSE and ancillary testing, and/or perform CNB or excision <span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">biopsy<sup>e,f</sup></span>.</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">CORRELATE, correlation of FNAB is required with clinical findings and with imaging, microbiological, serological, flow cytometric, immunocytochemical, and molecular findings whenever available.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">a</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">The range</span> of ROM is derived from a review of 10&nbsp;<a name=\"_Hlk176965421\"></a>studies <span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk176965421;\"><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{39097796}</span>, and the pooled ROM (in parentheses after the range) is from a meta-analysis </span><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk176965421;\"><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{38096796}</span>.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">b</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">These</span> recommendations are for further diagnostic workup of the lymph node to attain as specific a diagnosis as possible of the entity or neoplasm, and do not relate to treatment.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">c</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">The</span> range of neoplastic and non-neoplastic entities that can involve lymph nodes is wide, and further diagnostic management will depend on the overall clinical setting, the availability of CNB and ancillary tests, the accessibility of the site, and the nature of the lesion, e.g. lymphoma versus metastatic disease, superficial lymph node versus mediastinal lymph node.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">d</span></sup>It is mandatory to correlate the FNAB report with clinical findings and with findings from imaging, microbiology, serology, flow cytometry, immunocytochemistry, and molecular tests whenever these are available, although all of these investigations will not be required in every case.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">e</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">For</span> haematolymphoid tumours, when FNAB is the initial diagnostic approach, excision biopsy is frequently required, although the FNAB and ancillary tests supplemented as required by CNB may be diagnostic in many tumours and moderate the need for excision biopsy. For haematolymphoid tumour relapses or metastases from known or potentially new primary sites where material for ancillary testing is available, and for difficult-to-access sites or specific clinical settings, excision biopsy is optional.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">f</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">If</span> ancillary testing and/or CNB are not available, excision biopsy of the lesion is usually required.</p>",
